Hi Sebastien.

> Another thing I have noticed is that I am getting the following
> warning
> message at the completion of fit when using rat arrays:
> Warning message:
> In fitfcn(y) :
>  Ignoring a unit group when fitting probe-level model, because it
> has a
> ridiculously large number of data points: 6515x6 > 5000x1
>
> what does this warning mean?

So, remember before when I mentioned about the "options()":

 > options()$aroma.affymetrix.settings$models$RmaPlm
$medianPolishThreshold
[1] 500   6

$skipThreshold
[1] 5000    1

... basically, this means that aroma.affymetrix will skip any
probesets with >5000 probes and for any probesets with >500 probes, it
will do the estimation (same model) with median polish instead of a
robust linear model.

> I have realised that the annotations I have been using are incorrect
> so
> the information I mentioned regarding RT1-C113 is irrelavant.
> However, I
> would still like to know how to (if possible) change the options for
> probe skipping.

You could change the options.  For example:

 > settings <- getOption("aroma.affymetrix.settings")
 > settings$models$RmaPlm$skipThreshold <- c(600,6) # put in what you
want here
 > options(aroma.affymetrix.settings=settings)

I'm not sure that is what you want to do though.  There may be some
quite interesting genes that have more than 100 probes that you
wouldn't want to exclude.  One thing for certain you could do is
remove some of the control probesets.  For example, if you grab the
NetAffx CSV from Affy, you could select only the "main" probes and do
the fits only on those.  For example:

 > x <- read.csv("RaGene-1_0-st-
v1.na27.rn4.transcript.csv",skip=19,header=TRUE,stringsAsF=FALSE)
 > dim(x)
[1] 29215    18
 > table(x$category)

             control->affx control->bgp-
 >antigenomic                      main
                        22
45                     27342
            normgene->exon          normgene->intron  rescue->FLmRNA-
 >unmapped
                       399
1153                       254

 > table(x$category,x$total_probes>100)

                             FALSE  TRUE
   control->affx                 9    13
   control->bgp->antigenomic     3    42
   main                      27303    39
   normgene->exon              399     0
   normgene->intron           1153     0
   rescue->FLmRNA->unmapped    123   131

That is, you probably want to include the 39 "main" probesets that
have >100 probes.  You can do this right when you call 'fit':

plmTr <- RmaPlm(csN, verbose=verbose)
fit(plmTr, units=u, verbose=verbose)

... where you pick off the units that you want to fit.

Hope that helps.

Cheers,
Mark

